"hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1102,"We are Abu Musa Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, we are Ibn Abi Adi, we are Hisham, H. Ya’qub bin Ibrahim Al-Dawrqi told us, we are Yazid bin Harun. Hisham bin Abi Abdullah told us, on the authority of Yahya, on the authority of Abu Salamah, he said: I asked Aisha about the prayer of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace. And he greeted her, and she said: “He would pray thirteen rak’ahs, pray eight rak’ahs, then perform Witr, then pray two rak’ahs while sitting, so if he wanted to He kneels, gets up and kneels, and prays two rak’ahs between the call and the iqamah.” This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Musa. Al-Dawrqi said in his hadith: “He performs the Witr prayer with one rak’ah. When he says the takbir, he prays two rak’ahs sitting, and he prays two rak’ahs between the call to prayer and the Iqamah from ""Dawn"""
1103,"We are told by Ahmad bin Al-Miqdam Al-Ajli, we are Bishr, meaning Ibn Al-Mufaddal, we are Abu Maslamah, on the authority of Abu Nadra, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, He said: I visited my aunt Maimunah and it was the night before the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, got up and performed a long ritual of fasting. I performed ablution, then he got up to pray, so I stood up and performed ablution, then I came and stood next to him. When he knew that I wanted to pray with him, he took my hand and turned me away from him. His right hand, so he prayed nine or seven witr prayers, then he prayed two rak’ahs, and he laid on his side until I heard his voice, then the prayer was established and he went off and prayed.” Abu Bakr said: These are the two rak’ahs that Ibn Abbas mentioned in this report. It is possible that he meant the two rak’ahs that the Prophet May God’s prayers and peace be upon him, he prayed them after Witr, as Aisha reported, and it is possible that he meant by them the two rak’ahs of dawn which were He prays them before the obligatory prayer"
